Ivan and Imre Leader and Mark Walters}, year={2026}, eprint={2606.13472}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13472}, }\nSubmitted 11 Jun 2026\n\n# Generalised Prisms and Euclidean Ramsey Theory\n\nMI\n\nMaria-Romina Ivan\n\nIL\n\nImre Leader\n\nMW\n\nMark Walters\n\n## Abstract\n\nA finite subset $X$X of $\\mathbb R^d$R d is called Ramsey if for every $k$k there exists an $n$n such that whenever $\\mathbb R^n$R n is $k$k-coloured there exists a monochromatic congruent copy of $X$X. K\\v rí\\v z showed that if there is a soluble group of symmetries of $X$X that acts transitively on $X$X, then $X$X is Ramsey. Determining which sets are Ramsey is a major unsolved problem. In this paper we show that if there is a finite group of isometries of $\\mathbb R^d$R d that acts transitively on a set $X$X, and also on a set $Y$Y, then the `prism' formed by $X$X and $Y$Y in $\\mathbb R^{d+1}$R d+1 (meaning the set $X$X together with a translate of $Y$Y in the direction perpendicular to $\\mathbb R^d$R d) is itself contained in a finite set on which a group of isometries acts transitively. Moreover, if the initial group of isometries is soluble then so is the final group. This provides a new tool for generating Ramsey sets.\n\nView PaperPDF\n\n## BibTeX\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{ivan2026generalisedprismseuclideanramsey, title={Generalised Prisms and Euclidean Ramsey Theory}, author={Maria-Romina Ivan and Imre Leader and Mark Walters}, year={2026}, eprint={2606.13472}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13472}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDr Maria Ivan | Department of Pure Mathematics and Mathematical Statistics (https://www.dpmms.cam.ac.uk/node/1340)\nciteturn3search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Research Interests: Extremal Combinatorics, Ramsey Theory, Poset Saturation ... (doi: 10.48550/arxiv.2606.13472)\n\nGeneralised Prisms and Eucl