----------\n[2607.03315] A unified approach to cross-intersection problems with applications to Hilton--Milner type theorems and stability (https://slimjectvitamins.com/accel-vitamin-b-complex-capsules/?_=%2Fabs%2F2607.03315%23CD3DgejCa2b3OIySVEszRQc%3D)\nciteturn3search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; View a PDF of the paper titled A unified approach to cross-intersection problems with applications to Hilton--Milner type theorems and stability, by Jie Wen and Benjian Lv ... Cite as:  | arXiv:2607.03315 [math.CO]\n\n# Mathematics > Combinatorics\n\n[Submitted on 3 Jul 2026 (v1), last revised 29 Aug 2026 (this version, v3)]\n\n# Title:A unified approach to cross-intersection problems with applications to Hilton--Milner type theorems and stability\n\nAuthors:Jie Wen, Benjian Lv\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled A unified approach to cross-intersection problems with applications to Hilton--Milner type theorems and stability, by Jie Wen and Benjian Lv\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:We develop a new approach to cross-intersection problems in extremal set theory. The method builds on the iterative procedure introduced by Kupavskii and Zakharov (2024) and the $t$-cover method. It provides a flexible framework for deriving extremal and stability results for cross $t$-intersecting families. Our approach applies to a variety of combinatorial objects. As an application, we prove a product version of the seminal Erdős--Ko--Rado theorem for sufficiently spread set systems.\n> Two families $\\mathcal{F}$ and $\\mathcal{G}$ of $k$-subsets of $[n]$ are called cross $t$-intersecting if $|F\\cap G|\\geq t$ for all $F\\in\\mathcal{F}$ and $G\\in\\mathcal{G}$. We determine the families maximizing $\\min\\{|\\mathcal{F}|, |\\mathcal{G}|\\}$ for large $n$ and all $t\\ge2$, generalizing results of Mörs (1985) and Füredi (1995) for cross $1$-intersecting families. We then determine the families maximizing $|\\mathcal{F}||\\mathcal{G}|$ under the condition $\\max\\{|\\cap_{F\\in\\mathcal{F}}F|,|\\cap_{G\\in\\mathcal{G}}G|\\}<t$ for large $n$. This improves the bound obtained by Frankl and Wang (2024), and provides a characterization of extremal configurations.\n> For a family $\\mathcal{F}$ of subsets of $[n]$, we introduce its $t$-diversity $\\gamma_t(\\mathcal{F})$, defined as the minimum number of sets from $\\mathcal{F}$ not containing a fixed $t$-subset. This serves as a natural generalization of the important notion of diversity for $t=1$. We obtain a stability result via $\\gamma_t$, and determine the maximum of $\\min\\{\\gamma_t(\\mathcal{F}),\\gamma_t(\\mathcal{G})\\}$ for cross $t$-intersecting families $\\mathcal{F}$ and $\\mathcal{G}$. These yield new results for $t$-intersecting families, including a stability theorem towards a conjecture of Ellis, Keller and Lifshitz (2019), which may also be regarded as a $t$-intersection version, for large $n$, of an influential theorem of Frankl (1987).\n\nSubjects:  | Combinatorics (math.CO)\nMSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | 05D05\nCite as:  | arXiv:2607.03315 [math.CO]\n   | (or arXiv:2607.03315v3 [math.CO] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2607.03315 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Jie Wen [view email]\n[v1] Fri, 3 Jul 2026 13:32:48 UTC (42 KB)\n[v2] Thu, 27 Aug 2026 13:51:07 UTC (42 KB)\n[v3] Sat, 29 Aug 2026 14:19:23 UTC (43 KB)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBalanced Sperner families via the topological Tverberg theorem - Science Explorer Abstract (https://scixplorer.org/abs/2026arXiv260610885S/abstract)\nciteturn3search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; ## Balanced Sperner families via the topological Tverberg theorem ... Publication  | eprint arXiv:2606.10885\n\n## Balanced Sperner families via the topological Tverberg theorem\n\n### Authors\n\nShangguan, Chong;\n\nXu, Zixiang;\n\nYang, Yulin\n\nEprint\n\nFull Text Sources\n\nOther Resources\n\n### Abstract\n\nFor every prime power $r\\ge 2$, we show that any Sperner family $\\mathcal F\\subseteq 2^{[n]}$ with $|\\mathcal F|\\ge (r-1)n+1$ contains $r$ pairwise disjoint nonempty subfamilies whose unions are all equal and whose intersections are all equal. For $r=2$, this confirms a conjecture of Hegedüs, with the sharp threshold $n+1$. In this purely combinatorial problem, our proof combines a multilinear polynomial method, a continuity argument, and the topological Tverberg theorem.\n\n### Details\n\nPublication  | eprint arXiv:2606.10885\nPublication Date  | June 2026\nDOI  | 10.48550/arXiv.2606.10885\narXiv  | arXiv:2606.10885\nBibcode  | 2026arXiv260610885S\nCollection  | general\nKeyword  | Combinatorics\nE-Print Comment(s)  | http://arxiv.org/abs/2606.10885--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBalanced Sperner families via the topological Tverberg theorem | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/406874012_Balanced_Sperner_families_via_the_topological_Tverberg_theorem)\nciteturn3search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # Balanced Sperner families via the topological Tverberg theorem ... DOI:10.48550/arXiv.2606.10885\n\n# Balanced Sperner families via the topological Tverberg theorem\n\n  * June 2026\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2606.10885\n\nAuthors:\n\nChong Shangguan\n\nChong Shangguan\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nZixiang Xu\n\n  * Zhejiang University\n\nYulin Yang\n\nYulin Yang\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nFor every prime power $r\\ge 2$, we show that any Sperner family $\\mathcal F\\subseteq 2^{[n]}$ with $|\\mathcal F|\\ge (r-1)n+1$ contains r pairwise disjoint nonempty subfamilies whose unions are all equal and whose intersections are all equal. For r=2, this confirms a conjecture of Hegedüs, with the sharp thre